05:49.000 --> 05:50.360
A couple of things that might be new,

05:50.360 --> 05:52.400
if you haven't really worked with components before,

05:52.400 --> 05:54.680
is that this is what's called a self-closing tag.

05:54.680 --> 05:57.720
So this is the equivalent of doing this.

05:57.720 --> 06:00.040
It shows it's a little bit more succinct, because rather

06:00.040 --> 06:02.640
than duplicating it over, it's fairly common

06:02.640 --> 06:04.840
to just self-inclose like this.

06:04.840 --> 06:07.320
In addition, something else that View tries to get you to do,

06:07.320 --> 06:09.600
though, before we talk more about this,

06:09.600 --> 06:11.560
is you'll notice that earlier I had this error, which is

06:11.560 --> 06:13.800
from ESLint, in that we really try to recommend

06:13.800 --> 06:16.480
that you use multi-word component names.

06:16.480 --> 06:18.280
And the reason for that is because you never

06:18.280 --> 06:20.720
know when the HTML spec is going to add something.

06:20.720 --> 06:23.000
And they basically have, you know,

06:23.000 --> 06:25.520
they get first dibs when it comes to one-word components.

06:25.520 --> 06:28.760
So rather than basically run the risk of conflicting that,

06:28.760 --> 06:30.440
what I might do instead is I might go,

06:30.440 --> 06:32.680
I might call this like base counter.

06:32.680 --> 06:37.560
This is like the counter to rule all components.

06:37.560 --> 06:39.200
And so you'll notice here, though, that Volar,

06:39.200 --> 06:42.080
hey, it says, I noticed you're refactoring some stuff.

06:42.080 --> 06:43.760
And then you say, oh, what did you notice?

06:43.760 --> 06:46.320
And it says, look, I noticed that inside of here,

06:46.320 --> 06:48.920
you actually, like, it'll actually track the file path

06:48.920 --> 06:51.160
of the things that it's related to, which is pretty cool.

06:51.160 --> 06:52.360
So I can go ahead and apply that.

06:52.360 --> 06:54.440
And now you'll see that inside of app.view,

06:54.440 --> 06:56.080
it's gone ahead and actually renamed it correctly

06:56.080 --> 06:58.240
inside of my imports, which is a nice optimization

06:58.240 --> 06:59.400
that it does for us.

06:59.400 --> 07:01.000
But once again, I really would like

07:01.000 --> 07:03.960
to avoid the single-word naming.

07:03.960 --> 07:06.040
So I'm just going to do that like that.

07:06.080 --> 07:09.240
And then you'll see everything still works as expected.

07:09.240 --> 07:12.160
But another thing that View does for you

07:12.160 --> 07:14.560
in terms of extending upon HTML standards

07:14.560 --> 07:17.400
is you might not like the Pascal case, which

07:17.400 --> 07:20.000
is where you have the multi-word capitalized.

07:20.000 --> 07:23.760
You might say, I would rather just do the standard kebab

07:23.760 --> 07:25.360
case for HTML.

07:25.360 --> 07:27.640
And believe it or not, that actually just works.

07:27.640 --> 07:29.920
View will out of the box basically

07:29.920 --> 07:31.840
allow you to convert between two cases

07:31.840 --> 07:36.280
because it knows how to track the differences.

07:36.280 --> 07:39.560
And so to say which one is better

07:39.560 --> 07:42.320
depends a lot on the team that you're working with.

07:42.320 --> 07:44.960
I would say in terms of pros and cons,

07:44.960 --> 07:47.440
one of the nice things about the Pascal case

07:47.440 --> 07:49.560
is that it becomes very evident that this is something very

07:49.560 --> 07:54.240
specific to the app, that this is something custom.

07:54.240 --> 07:56.800
Granted, if you're using a multi-word component,

07:56.800 --> 07:59.480
that also does the job as well.

07:59.480 --> 08:01.920
So this is kind of what you're used to in that regard.

